# Copy this file to ".env" and adjust for your setup, then start the API with
# `node server.js` from api/ and serve the built frontend from frontend/dist/.
#
# ── Local testing (default) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `npm run dev` in frontend/ serves on 5173 and proxies /api to the API on 3000.
RP_ID=localhost
ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173
RP_NAME=FIT'ONE

# ── Real deployment behind your own HTTPS domain ───────────────────────────
# Passkeys are bound to the exact hostname, and browsers only allow them over
# HTTPS (localhost is the one exception). ORIGIN must be the address people
# actually type — the same origin that serves the app, because passkeys and the
# session cookie both require it:
#
#   RP_ID=coach.example.com
#   ORIGIN=https://coach.example.com
#   RP_NAME=FIT'ONE
#
# Your web server serves frontend/dist/ and proxies /api to the API process.
# See docs/SELF_HOSTING.md.

# ── Admin dashboard & invite-only signup (optional) ────────────────────────
# Both off by default — a fresh instance has open signup and no admin.
#
# Make yourself an admin: register your own passkey profile first, find your
# user id in ./data/db.json ("users"[].id), then list it here (comma-separated
# for several admins). Admins get an "Admin dashboard" link in Settings to see
# users, disable accounts, generate invite codes and view workout history.
#
#   ADMIN_UIDS=youruserid,anotheradmin
#
# Require an invite code to create new profiles (existing accounts keep working;
# generate the codes from the admin dashboard):
#
#   INVITE_ONLY=1
#
# On a brand-new instance that is a closed loop: registering needs an invite,
# invites are minted only by an admin, and admins are recognised only by
# ADMIN_UIDS — so nobody, including you, can get in. Set a bootstrap code and
# enter it as the invite code when you create the first profile; that account
# is made an admin. Needs INVITE_ONLY=1 to be usable at all — that is what makes
# the sign-in form ask for a code. Use a long random string; under 16 characters
# it is ignored with a warning, and it is matched case-insensitively:
#
#   BOOTSTRAP_CODE=$(openssl rand -hex 16)
#
# Clear it once you are in. It disarms as soon as a user exists, but that is a
# live check against the current user count rather than a one-time event, so an
# instance whose DATA_DIR is lost or restored from an empty backup re-arms while
# the variable is still set.
#
# Remove the "Continue without account" button, so a profile is the only way in.
# Guest mode keeps everything in the browser and never touches this server, but
# on an instance meant for a known set of people it is still the wrong front
# door. Anyone already inside as a guest is signed out on their next visit; the
# data they made stays in that browser. Defaults to on (guests allowed):
#
#   ALLOW_GUEST=0

# Let visitors without an invite code ask for one from the sign-in screen
# ("Request access": name + phone + optional note). Requests land in the admin
# dashboard; approving one mints an invite code you send to the person yourself
# (the app never contacts them). Pending requests expire after 14 days. This is
# the app's only unauthenticated write endpoint — the app itself has no rate
# limiting by design, so if your instance faces the open internet, rate-limit
# POST /api/access/request at your reverse proxy. Off by default:
#
#   REQUEST_ACCESS=1
# ── Activity log (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The admin dashboard shows an activity log: who signed in, who tried and
# failed, and every admin action (disabling an account, creating or revoking an
# invite code). It lives in ./data/audit.log, one JSON object per line, so you
# can also just read it with `tail` or `jq`.
#
# It is ON by default. It records strictly less than this instance already
# holds — every profile is in db.json and every workout is in state-<uid>.json,
# both readable by any admin — and a log that ships switched off tells you
# nothing on the day you need it. Turn it off completely with:
#
#   AUDIT_LOG=0
#
# Retention is a cap, not an archive. Whichever limit is reached first wins;
# set either to 0 to disable that limit:
#
#   AUDIT_MAX=5000            # events kept
#   AUDIT_DAYS=90             # days kept
#
# IP addresses are the one thing that is OFF unless you ask, because they are
# the only field here that says where somebody physically is. They are also the
# only way to tell one failed sign-in from twenty. Three settings:
#
#   AUDIT_IP=off              # default — no addresses at all
#   AUDIT_IP=net              # network only: 203.0.113.0/24, 2001:db8:1::/48
#   AUDIT_IP=full             # the whole address
#
# The address is read from CF-Connecting-IP, then the first entry of
# X-Forwarded-For, then X-Real-IP, and finally the connecting socket itself if
# none of those is present.
#
# A forwarded-for header is only worth anything if whatever sits in front
# OVERWRITES it instead of passing a client-supplied one through — otherwise a
# caller simply states the address it would like logged. The web container now
# overwrites X-Forwarded-For and X-Real-IP with the real peer, and DROPS
# CF-Connecting-IP, so the bundled stack records an address nobody can choose.
# (Before, X-Forwarded-For was appended to rather than replaced, and
# CF-Connecting-IP was passed straight through — both were forgeable.)
#
# Cloudflare is first in that list on purpose: a Cloudflare tunnel does not put
# the visitor in X-Forwarded-For at all, so that header would otherwise record
# the tunnel itself and look perfectly plausible. If — and only if — Cloudflare
# is genuinely in front of you, let its header through again:
#
#   CF_CONNECTING_IP=$http_cf_connecting_ip
#
# That is safe there because Cloudflare sets the header itself, replacing
# anything the client sent. Anywhere else, leave it unset.

# ── Session length (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# How long a sign-in lasts, in days. Default 90 — long enough that people who
# train regularly never see a login screen, short enough that a stolen cookie
# doesn't stay good forever. Lower it for an instance on the open internet,
# raise it for a private one. Only affects new sign-ins; cookies already handed
# out keep the lifetime they were issued with. Users can end every session on
# every device themselves with "Sign out everywhere" in Settings.
#
#   SESSION_DAYS=90

# ── Uploaded media size caps (optional) ────────────────────────────────────
# Coach exercise demos and progress photos, in MB. These are separate from the
# 5 MB JSON body limit on purpose: uploads stream straight to disk instead of
# being buffered and parsed, so a large video is never a large Buffer in the
# node heap, and no JSON route inherits the bigger number.
#
# The video default is 90 rather than something rounder because it is chosen by
# whatever sits in FRONT of the app, not by what node would accept. Cloudflare
# refuses request bodies over 100 MB on its free and pro plans, and a cap above
# the edge's is not a cap — the upload dies out there with an HTML error page
# the app cannot parse, while the app believes it enforced a limit it never
# saw. If you front this with nginx, raise both together (nginx's
# `client_max_body_size` and this) or the smaller one silently wins.
#
# Files land in DATA_DIR/media/, so they are covered by the same backup as
# everything else — see the backup note above.
#
# Your proxy's own body limit must stay ABOVE the video cap. It is a backstop,
# never the intended one to fire: node answers a 413 in JSON the app can read, a
# proxy answers with an HTML page the app cannot parse and reports an error that
# has nothing to do with size. Raising MEDIA_MAX_VIDEO_MB without raising your
# proxy's limit makes the proxy the real ceiling.
#
# MEDIA_MAX_PHOTO_MB bounds a client's own progress photos (POST /api/media/photo).
# 12 MB is generous on purpose and is not the number that normally applies: the
# app re-draws every photo on a canvas at 1600 px before sending it, which lands
# around 300-500 KB. The cap is there for the raw-bytes path — the route takes a
# file, not a form, so anything can post to it — and for a phone the resize
# failed on.
#
#   MEDIA_MAX_VIDEO_MB=90
#   MEDIA_MAX_PHOTO_MB=12

# ── The port the API listens on (optional) ─────────────────────────────────
# 3000 unless you say otherwise. Whatever you pick, your web server has to
# proxy /api to it — the app and the API must be one origin, or passkeys and
# the session cookie both break.
#
#   PORT=3000

# ── Notifications (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Push works out of the box — nothing to set. VAPID keys are generated on first
# run and saved to ./data/vapid.json. Each user's browser reports its own
# timezone automatically, so the workout-day reminder fires at their local
# time regardless of where the server runs or where they're travelling.
#
# Push services want a contact address for whoever operates the server, in case
# they need to get in touch about your pushes. Defaults to your ORIGIN, which is
# fine; set a mailto: if you would rather they mailed you:
#
#   VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com

# ── Hevy map regeneration (developers only) ────────────────────────────────
# Not used by the running app. End users paste their own key in Settings →
# Import from Hevy. Set this only to regenerate frontend/src/lib/hevy-id-map.js:
#
#   HEVY_API_KEY=
#   node scripts/build-hevy-id-map.mjs
